<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Guadalquivir</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Baetis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Guadalquivir is the main river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andalusia">Andalusia</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, flowing about 650 km west from the Sierra de Cazorla past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> to the Atlantic at Sanlúcar de Barrameda. Called the Baetis by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, it gave its name to the province of Baetica. Navigable up to Seville, it made that city the port of Spain's trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guadalquivir.jpg" alt="Guadalquivir" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Phillip Capper · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Guadalquivir1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
